{"sequence":235,"type":"assistant_text","sourceId":"conversation:0124e133-1eb8-4971-bc5c-65bdd71e4505:text:0","timestamp":"2026-09-29T20:18:48.472Z","text":"Now that I have the real API shape, this maps onto our rubric nicely — and confirms skipping both community SDKs was the right call, since `java.net.http.HttpClient` (built into the JDK) plus the Jackson dependency already in `pom.xml` is genuinely all you'd need.\n\n**1. Config (`Config.java`)** — three new env vars, only required when this judge is selected: `JEV_API_KEY`, `JEV_BASE_URL` (default `https://api.typesafe.ai`), `JEV_MODEL` (default `jev-latest`).\n\n**2. Extract a `Scorer` interface.** Right now `BenchmarkRunner`'s constructor is hard-typed to the concrete `LlmJudgeScorer` (`BenchmarkRunner.java:28`). To plug in a second judge, pull out `interface Scorer { ScoredAnswer score(BenchmarkQuestion q, String actualAnswer); }`, have `LlmJudgeScorer implements Scorer`, and change `BenchmarkRunner`'s constructor to accept `Scorer` — same shape as the `ContentRetriever` swap we already did for GraphRAG vs. vector-only.\n\n**3. New `TypeSafeJudgeScorer implements Scorer`.** One `POST /v1/systemone` call per question, mapping our rubric onto Jev's typed questions instead of a free-text prompt:\n- `state`: a small JSON object — `{question, category, unanswerable, goldAnswer, actualAnswer}` — replacing today's hand-templated prompt string entirely.\n- `questions.correctness`: a `score` question, 3 levels — `[\"wrong or missing the key facts\", \"partially correct\", \"fully correct and consistent with the gold reference\"]`.\n- `questions.faithfulness`: a `score` question, 3 levels mirroring the current faithfulness rubric.\n- `questions.refusal`: a `noul` (yes/no) question, e.g. \"Did the system plainly decline rather than fabricate an answer?\"\n- Parse the response, map `score`/`noul` values back into `ScoredAnswer`'s ints/boolean, with manual 429/529 exponential backoff (the docs note SDKs handle this automatically — since we're skipping the SDK, that retry loop is ours to write, though it's small).\n\n**4. `Main.java` wiring** — extend the existing arg-based selection pattern (it already picks `seed`/`vector`/default) to also pick the judge, defaulting to the current OpenAI judge and adding an opt-in for Jev, since the ask was an *additional* run, not a replacement. Report/CSV filenames get the judge name baked in, same as we discussed for the naming scheme.\n\n**Two real design decisions this surfaces, not just plumbing** — worth you weighing in on before this gets built:\n\n- **No rationale field.** Jev returns typed probabilities/confidence, never free text — there's no equivalent to today's one-sentence `judge:` explanation in the report file you asked for last time. Options: leave it blank for Jev-judged rows, or synthesize a pseudo-rationale from the returned `probabilities`/`confidence`/`legend` (e.g. \"correctness: 78% fully-correct vs. 15% partially-correct\") — arguably more informative than a sentence, but different in kind.\n- **Score-to-int mapping.** Jev's `score` field is a *probability-weighted average* across levels (e.g. `1.35`), not a clean 0/1/2. Round the weighted value, or take the single highest-probability level from `probabilities` instead? Rounding a weighted average can overstate confidence near the boundary; the argmax is more honest but throws away the \"how split was it\" signal.\n\nLet me know how you'd want those two handled, and whether you want this actually built now or still just scoped."}